description:
Spawn a Blueprint node by its menu name and get its REAL pins back — the preferred way to add macros, casts, switches, flow-control, and function-call nodes WITHOUT hand-authoring T3D.

Use this instead of write_blueprint for any node whose exact T3D you would otherwise guess: ForEachLoop / ForLoop / WhileLoop / DoOnce / Gate / FlipFlop / Sequence (macros), Branch, Cast nodes, Switch on Int/Enum, MakeArray, delegate add/call, and ordinary function calls. The node is created, compiled, and its actual pin names/types are returned so you can wire it with edit_blueprint.

To reference a COMPONENT (the editor's drag-a-component-from-the-Components-panel getter), pass "Get <ComponentName>" — this works for components inherited from a C++ parent or parent Blueprint too (e.g. a CameraSpringArm declared in C++), even when there is no menu action for them. Use this instead of GetComponentByClass + Cast. The inherited component names are listed in read_blueprint's overview Components section.

When the menu name or node class is uncertain, use search_nodes first (discover_tools category "discovery_tools") and copy its concrete action name/class. Keyboard input events are named by the key, for example node:"W"; configured input actions/axes use their mapping names. A generic event category is not a spawnable action. Preserve existing mappings.

After insert_node: wire the new node with edit_blueprint using the returned pin NAMES. Do NOT re-create it with write_blueprint (that would duplicate it).

connect_from wires an existing node's exec output into the new node. IMPORTANT: an exec output holds only ONE link, so if that pin was already connected its old link is broken (the result message tells you). To INSERT INTO an existing chain (A -> B becomes A -> new -> B), pass splice=true — the old target is re-wired to the new node's 'then' (or first exec output) automatically, no manual disconnect/reconnect needed.

connect_to is the mirror of connect_from: it wires this NEW node's first compatible OUTPUT into an existing node's input (exec, pose, or data — the schema picks the pair; omit the target pin to auto-pick). PREFER connect_from / connect_to over inserting then running a separate edit_blueprint connect: a node wired at insert time lands next to the node it feeds, instead of stranded across the canvas. (If you do insert unwired then connect later, the node is still pulled next to its peer on that first wire.)

Examples:
- {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "ForEachLoop"}
- {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "Branch", "connect_from": {"node": "K2Node_Event_0", "pin": "then"}}
- feed a downstream node: {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "Get bIsInAir", "connect_to": {"node": "K2Node_CallFunction_4", "pin": "NewParam"}}
- insert between two connected nodes: {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "Print String", "connect_from": {"node": "K2Node_CallFunction_3", "pin": "then", "splice": true}}
- {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "GetActorLocation", "class": "Actor"}
- {"blueprint": "/Game/BP_X", "node": "Get CameraSpringArm"}

ANIMATION BLUEPRINTS: in an anim graph (graph addresses like "AnimGraph" or a nested state's bound graph), node names are anim nodes — "Blend", "Two Way Blend", "Layered blend per bone", "State Machine", "Slot 'DefaultSlot'", IK nodes, or "Play 'AssetName'" for an asset player (skeleton-checked against the Blueprint's target skeleton). Pins include pose pins reported with pose_space ("local" or "component"); wire pose output → pose input. connect_from in an anim graph wires the source's POSE output into the new node's first POSE input (not exec); the default source pin is the node's pose output, and pose inputs auto-break any prior link (no splice).

Search the project's Asset Registry for Blueprints, structs, enums, data tables, materials, and other assets.

Examples:
- Find all Blueprints: {"class_filter": "Blueprint"}
- Find in folder: {"class_filter": "Blueprint", "path": "/Game/Characters"}
- Find by name: {"query": "Enemy"}
- Find structs: {"class_filter": "UserDefinedStruct"}
- Filter by parent: {"class_filter": "Blueprint", "parent_class": "Character"}

Read permitted C++ source files with bounded work and explicit partial results. Files over 1 MiB are refused without loading; output is capped at 32 KiB including the result envelope.

Modes:
- "find": Search for source files by name pattern. Returns bounded pages of paths and traversal metrics. Pass the returned cursor with identical pattern/scope/limit to continue. Live tree changes may omit or duplicate entries; expired cursors require restarting.
- "outline" (default): Extract UCLASS/UFUNCTION/UPROPERTY declarations with doc comments from a header. Skips function bodies. Use this to understand a class.
- "raw": Raw file content with line-range pagination. Use for .cpp implementation details.

Workflow:
1. find to locate files: {"mode": "find", "pattern": "PGPawn*"}
2. outline to understand the class: {"mode": "outline", "file": "/path/to/PGPawn.h"}
3. raw for implementation: {"mode": "raw", "file": "/path/to/PGPawn.cpp", "offset": 100, "limit": 200}

Examples:
- Find project files: {"mode": "find", "pattern": "PGPawn*"}
- Find engine class: {"mode": "find", "pattern": "Actor.h", "scope": "engine"}
- Outline a header: {"mode": "outline", "file": "/full/path/to/PGPawn.h"}
- Filter outline: {"mode": "outline", "file": "/path/to/Actor.h", "query": "Location"}
- Read lines: {"mode": "raw", "file": "/path/to/PGPawn.cpp", "offset": 0, "limit": 200}
